  "query_preview": "..(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 255 PM EST WED NOV 18 2026\n\nThere were no significant changes in the long term forecast compared \nto previous discussions, especially through the remainder of this \nweek. Friday and Saturday look to remain dry across the forecast \narea with highs in the mid 60s to around 70 as high pressure \ngenerally dominates. A gradual increase in clouds Friday and \nSaturday is possible ahead of the next cold front and shortwave from \nlate on Sunday into Monday. Lows Friday night should trend warmer \ncompared to the near term period with another ridge/valley \ntemperature split with mid 30s eastern valleys to mid 40s on the \nridges. Further moderation in lows is anticipated for Saturday \nnight as the front and shortwave begin to approach. \n\nOur attention then turns to the Sunday to Monday timeframe when the \ncold front is progged to move across the CWA. Model agreement with \nthe evolution of this system and its associated rain chances are \nless than optimal at the moment. Guidance is in agreement in the \npresence of the trough traversing far to our north across the Great \nLakes this weekend, but that's about it. It appears that the \nprobabilities for widespread rain starts to dwindle as you go south \naway from the surface low (which looks like it will also track over \nthe northern Great lakes). The NBM, Euro, and GFS have total qpf for \nthis system generally in the trace to 0.1\" range. The 00Z LREF \nmean was a bit more aggressive with qpf, but is still only \nsuggesting 0.20\" to 0.25\". To throw another wrench into the mix, \nand possibly explain for the model spread, an extratropical system\nlooks to move ashore in the Carolinas around the same time this \nweekend. This system/low will likely be quite a bit stronger than \nthe low in the Great Lakes and may affect rain chances and timing \nto some degree. The bottom line, isolated to scattered PoPs \n(20-40%) are still forecast with the cold front crossing the area\nin the Sunday night into Monday timeframe. Considerable \nuncertainty exists in the finer details like rainfall totals. \n\nThanks to the aforementioned cold front, next week should open a bit \ncooler and quieter. Look for highs in the first half of the week to \nrange from the mid-40s to mid-50s. Lows trend back down to the low-\nmid 30s. We are also still keeping an eye on the potential for the \npattern to become active again starting sometime in the latter half \nof next week. Teleconnections look to line up in their phases that \nusually allow for meridional jet and multiple waves/impulses leading \nto multiple precip chances. Global models and ensembles are also \nstill showing that active jet and troughing across the US starting \nlater next week. These teleconnection phases also align with \nincreased probabilities for below average temps. Now, an important \nnote is that average highs here at JKL in November is about 57, so \nbelow average temperatures will likely not mean winter-like cold \nquite yet. Either way, the signals are still there for a more active \npattern in the eastern half of the US starting later next week and \nit's something worth keeping an eye on.",
